WEBVTT

00:01.877 --> 00:03.044
That concludes...

00:03.046 --> 00:09.760
...the detailed data on Kiyotaka Ayanokoji
and the 156 second-year students.

00:09.760 --> 00:12.304
You've memorized it all, haven't you?

00:12.304 --> 00:17.476
Your mission is to have
Ayanokoji expelled by April...

00:17.476 --> 00:20.646
...and bring him back to the White Room.

00:20.646 --> 00:23.815
However, carry this out discreetly.

00:23.815 --> 00:27.486
If this becomes public,
it could damage that person's...

00:27.486 --> 00:31.198
I mean, the teacher's reputation.

00:32.950 --> 00:36.729
You don't seem convinced.

00:37.695 --> 00:42.267
Is it because you don't like how
he's being praised as the masterpiece?

00:42.960 --> 00:46.129
Well then, the stage has been set.

00:46.129 --> 00:49.508
All that remains is for you
to give it your all.

00:49.508 --> 00:54.972
With the level of skill you possess,
expelling him should be a piece of cake.

00:56.056 --> 00:59.251
There's one thing I forgot to confirm.

00:59.726 --> 01:03.213
You're not hiding anything
from me, are you?

01:10.070 --> 01:13.015
A battle between
White Room students...

01:13.490 --> 01:15.993
Hunt or be hunted...

01:16.535 --> 01:18.996
It's just a game between children...

01:19.997 --> 01:23.275
...but it looks like it's going
to be interesting.

01:28.505 --> 01:32.663
Look, the blackboard has turned
into a monitor! Isn't that amazing?

01:32.665 --> 01:34.405
Sup, Ayanokoji.

01:34.407 --> 01:35.514
Miyake, huh.

01:36.680 --> 01:40.559
I was a bit worried since you
didn't show up much with the group

01:40.559 --> 01:42.086
during spring break.

01:42.519 --> 01:43.852
Sorry.

01:43.854 --> 01:45.406
I was a bit busy back then.

01:45.408 --> 01:48.231
Of course, you don't have
to force yourself to join,

01:48.233 --> 01:53.055
but there's Haruka, and more than
anything, Airi seemed worried about you.

01:55.240 --> 01:56.617
Sorry about that.

01:56.617 --> 01:59.286
I'll try to hang out
with you guys more often.

01:59.286 --> 02:00.912
That's good, then.

02:00.912 --> 02:03.189
I was feeling lonely without
you around myself.

02:03.790 --> 02:05.715
What were you guys talking about?

02:05.717 --> 02:06.753
Nothing.

02:07.669 --> 02:09.420
S-Suzune?!

02:09.420 --> 02:11.965
What did you do to your hair?!

02:11.965 --> 02:14.926
Horikita, you've had
a complete makeover.

02:14.926 --> 02:18.178
Is it really that strange
that I cut my hair?

02:18.180 --> 02:22.891
N-No... It's not strange at all,
it's just surprising.

02:22.893 --> 02:25.437
I mean, short hair
actually suits you...

02:25.437 --> 02:27.439
R-Right, Kushida?

02:27.439 --> 02:31.318
Yeah, you're right.
But did something happen?

02:31.318 --> 02:34.256
If I had to say,
it's a declaration of my resolve.

02:34.258 --> 02:36.031
Now that we're second-year students,

02:36.031 --> 02:39.326
we'll be fighting to move
Class D up to a higher class.

02:39.326 --> 02:42.704
And I want to do what I can
to make that happen.

02:42.704 --> 02:44.414
I see.

02:44.414 --> 02:48.460
Then maybe I'll try growing my hair out.

02:48.460 --> 02:50.962
You guys are noisy.

02:50.962 --> 02:52.739
Hurry up and take your seats.

02:53.715 --> 02:55.926
Without further ado,

02:55.926 --> 03:00.722
I need you to complete an important
procedure for your future school life.

03:00.722 --> 03:06.586
Access the school website
and install a new application.

03:07.410 --> 03:11.108
The app's official name is
"Over All Ability,"

03:13.151 --> 03:15.737
or "OAA" for short.

03:15.737 --> 03:17.489
OAA?

03:17.489 --> 03:20.701
This app, which will be
introduced to all grades,

03:20.701 --> 03:25.205
will provide various benefits
for your school life.

03:25.205 --> 03:29.626
For example, it contains
personal data for all students.

03:29.626 --> 03:31.628
Try checking your own data.

03:31.628 --> 03:37.384
M-Ms. Chabashira, my records
look like stats in a video game!

03:37.384 --> 03:38.885
That's right.

03:38.885 --> 03:41.763
Those are your individual records
calculated by the school...

03:41.763 --> 03:45.142
...based on your achievements
up to the end of your first year.

03:45.142 --> 03:50.147
Regardless of class, you can view the
records of all students across all grades.

03:50.147 --> 03:53.400
My academic ability score is E?

03:53.400 --> 03:56.528
Scores will be updated
at the beginning of each month.

03:56.528 --> 03:59.114
Sudo, even if your score is E now,

03:59.114 --> 04:04.161
if you score full marks on the next
written exam, you will be given an A+.

04:04.161 --> 04:05.662
Ooh!

04:05.662 --> 04:10.041
But does that mean our bad records
will remain as a stain on us?

04:10.041 --> 04:13.170
And everyone in the school
can see them too?

04:13.170 --> 04:16.798
Your first-year records are nothing
more than records of the past.

04:16.798 --> 04:20.177
They will not affect your future scores.

04:20.178 --> 04:25.307
In other words, the school hopes this will
help those with poor grades reevaluate...

04:25.307 --> 04:27.684
...and encourage their growth.

04:27.684 --> 04:30.312
<i>A convenient tool
for checking records...</i>

04:30.979 --> 04:33.315
<i>No, it can't be just that.</i>

04:33.315 --> 04:35.609
This app is impartial.

04:35.609 --> 04:37.736
Everyone is assessed equally,

04:37.736 --> 04:39.321
and it will also serve as a tool

04:39.321 --> 04:42.908
to promote interaction
regardless of grade level.

04:42.908 --> 04:47.078
However, I believe that's not all.

04:47.913 --> 04:50.248
This is just my personal
speculation, but...

04:50.248 --> 04:55.212
Students whose overall ability does not
meet a certain standard within a year...

04:55.212 --> 04:58.548
...may face some kind of penalty.

04:58.548 --> 05:00.342
A penalty?

05:00.342 --> 05:02.552
Could it be expulsion?

05:02.552 --> 05:04.846
Y-You're kidding, right?!

05:04.846 --> 05:08.099
Some of you may not be
satisfied with your scores,

05:08.099 --> 05:11.228
but this is your current evaluation.

05:11.228 --> 05:12.562
If you're unhappy with it,

05:12.562 --> 05:14.648
then show what you've got
over the next year,

05:14.648 --> 05:16.858
to convince the school
to change its mind.

05:17.734 --> 05:19.861
It's like an individual battle.

05:19.861 --> 05:22.697
<i>The introduction of this app
seems to overturn...</i>

05:22.697 --> 05:25.356
<i>...the way we've fought
as a class up until now.</i>

05:26.117 --> 05:28.620
<i>It's no wonder students
are confused and dissatisfied.</i>

05:28.620 --> 05:30.539
For the record, this system...

05:30.539 --> 05:35.377
...was proposed by the current student
council president, Miyabi Nagumo.

05:35.377 --> 05:38.964
<i>So Nagumo's dream of creating
a system where individuals...</i>

05:38.964 --> 05:43.134
<i>...are assessed on their own
merits has been realized.</i>

05:43.134 --> 05:48.765
Your first special exam as second-year
students will also introduce...

05:48.765 --> 05:53.103
...a new approach
unlike anything before.

05:53.103 --> 05:58.400
It's a written exam where you,
the second-years,

05:58.400 --> 06:01.611
will be paired with incoming
first-year students.

06:01.977 --> 06:04.521
Paired with first-year students?

06:04.990 --> 06:06.324
Until now, the school

06:06.324 --> 06:09.953
hasn't encouraged deep interaction
between different grade levels.

06:09.953 --> 06:10.996
Which is why,

06:10.996 --> 06:14.499
it has determined that students'
communication skills have been lacking.

06:14.499 --> 06:19.045
B-But we're still competing against
students in our own grade, right?

06:19.045 --> 06:21.840
It just feels kind of off...

06:21.840 --> 06:23.300
Think about it.

06:23.300 --> 06:26.303
When you enter the workforce,
the people you interact with...

06:26.303 --> 06:29.014
...won't all be fellow new graduates.

06:29.014 --> 06:30.473
There may even be situations

06:30.473 --> 06:34.269
where you compete with
first-years or third-years as well.

06:34.269 --> 06:38.481
You're free to partner with
anyone from any first-year class.

06:38.481 --> 06:40.859
The exam will take place two weeks
from now, at the end of the month.

06:40.859 --> 06:45.030
You'll have plenty of time
to find your partner.

06:45.429 --> 06:47.699
<i>Building trust from scratch</i>

06:47.699 --> 06:51.036
<i>with underclassmen
you barely know is difficult.</i>

06:51.036 --> 06:54.289
<i>That's where the OAA app comes in.</i>

06:54.289 --> 06:57.751
The test will cover five subjects,
with a total score of 500 points.

06:58.543 --> 07:00.843
As for the important rules,

07:00.845 --> 07:07.302
there are two areas in which you'll be
competing: as a class and as individuals.

07:07.302 --> 07:10.305
So there really is
an expulsion penalty!

07:10.305 --> 07:12.999
Partnerships are formed
by mutual consent,

07:13.001 --> 07:17.020
and you can't dissolve them
for any reason.

07:17.020 --> 07:18.772
You can already see it, right?

07:18.772 --> 07:23.193
In this exam, students with higher
academic ratings will be chosen first.

07:23.818 --> 07:28.823
Ms. Chabashira, what happens to students
who can't find a partner before the exam?

07:29.824 --> 07:33.787
If you're left unchosen, a pair will be
assigned randomly on the day of the test.

07:33.787 --> 07:38.833
However, you'll be subject to a penalty
of 5% of your total score.

07:38.833 --> 07:42.087
So that means we're screwed
if we don't find a partner...

07:46.091 --> 07:48.385
W-What should we do, Hirata?!

07:48.385 --> 07:49.636
My academic ranking is E!

07:49.636 --> 07:52.514
There's no way any underclassman
would want to pair with me!

07:52.514 --> 07:54.724
I'm not very confident either...

07:54.724 --> 07:56.476
All right, calm down first.

07:56.476 --> 07:58.395
Then let's come up with a plan.

07:58.395 --> 08:01.773
That's right.
There's no need to panic.

08:01.773 --> 08:04.776
Take a look at the exam overview
and the predicted scores...

08:04.776 --> 08:07.404
...based on academic level
sent to the app.

08:08.029 --> 08:10.824
The cutoff for expulsion
is a total of 500 points.

08:10.824 --> 08:15.120
In other words, even a student with
an E ranking can feel safe...

08:15.120 --> 08:17.622
...if they pair up with a student
ranked B or higher.

08:17.622 --> 08:20.000
Just like Horikita said.

08:20.000 --> 08:24.379
If we all work together, we can
get through this exam without a hitch.

08:24.379 --> 08:28.299
What's important is not
to choose a partner carelessly.

08:28.299 --> 08:32.136
If anything happens, be sure
to consult either me or Hirata.

08:34.139 --> 08:38.058
<i>If a student is judged to have
intentionally scored low,</i>

08:38.058 --> 08:40.682
<i>they'll be expelled regardless
of their grade, huh?</i>

08:41.479 --> 08:44.399
<i>Normally, that would be more
than enough as a deterrent.</i>

08:44.399 --> 08:48.027
<i>But for a White Room student,
it's a different story.</i>

08:49.112 --> 08:51.781
<i>If they manage to pair up with me,</i>

08:51.781 --> 08:55.274
<i>they wouldn't hesitate to drag me down
with them and score zero.</i>

08:56.014 --> 08:57.086
<i>In other words,</i>

08:57.088 --> 09:00.874
<i>if I end up choosing someone
from the White Room as my partner,</i>

09:00.874 --> 09:03.001
<i>that alone would be enough
to get me expelled.</i>

09:03.001 --> 09:05.587
<i>Acting Chairman Tsukishiro...</i>

09:05.587 --> 09:09.007
<i>There's no doubt this rule
was created by that man.</i>

09:14.345 --> 09:16.014
Do you need something from me?

09:16.014 --> 09:18.475
You remember our promise
from winter break,

09:18.475 --> 09:21.144
that you and I would compete, right?

09:21.644 --> 09:25.106
So this special exam is
the perfect opportunity, huh?

09:25.106 --> 09:26.274
Yes.

09:26.274 --> 09:29.277
If you win,
I'll join the student council.

09:29.277 --> 09:31.112
But if I win,

09:31.362 --> 09:35.116
you'll have to put the skills you've been
hiding to use for the sake of the class.

09:35.436 --> 09:37.118
I have no problem with that.

09:37.118 --> 09:38.906
That's good.

09:38.908 --> 09:41.640
Then let's move on.

09:42.499 --> 09:46.836
I looked into the first-years
with academic ability of B or higher.

09:46.836 --> 09:53.281
There are 17 in Class A, 13 each in
Classes B and C, and 11 in Class D.

09:53.885 --> 09:55.887
That makes 54 in total.

09:56.638 --> 10:00.391
In our class, there are
only 4 students with an E score,

10:00.391 --> 10:03.311
and even including D,
there are just 12.

10:03.311 --> 10:07.148
The first-years have more
than enough capable students.

10:07.148 --> 10:13.613
The problem is how many of those top
students we can pair with D or E students.

10:13.613 --> 10:14.614
Exactly.

10:14.614 --> 10:18.868
Among them, I think Sudo is the
one we should prioritize.

10:18.868 --> 10:23.373
Hasn't Sudo's academic
ability improved quite a bit?

10:23.373 --> 10:25.291
Academically, yes.

10:25.291 --> 10:28.545
But if you were a first-year
who didn't know him,

10:28.545 --> 10:31.631
would you pair with Sudo or Ike?

10:31.631 --> 10:35.301
True. With his intimidating
build and harsh tone,

10:35.301 --> 10:38.388
Sudo definitely gives off
a scary impression.

10:38.388 --> 10:39.472
Yes.

10:39.472 --> 10:43.351
If things go badly, he might even
struggle just to find a partner.

10:43.351 --> 10:46.896
That's why I want to act immediately.
Will you help me?

10:51.317 --> 10:53.403
That's Ichinose for you.

10:53.403 --> 10:58.408
She's thinking not just about Class B,
but about everyone as a whole.

10:58.408 --> 11:02.912
Class A and Class C will probably
come up with their own strategies too.

11:02.912 --> 11:06.666
We don't know what Ryuen
or Sakayanagi will do yet.

11:06.666 --> 11:12.046
But I think Private Points
will be a key factor.

11:12.046 --> 11:15.216
In other words, a strategy of
buying people off with money.

11:15.216 --> 11:16.009
Yes.

11:16.009 --> 11:19.804
For first-years, points are the most
straightforward form of value.

11:19.804 --> 11:25.226
We should also invest some funds
and secure a certain number of students.

11:25.226 --> 11:27.812
A money-based strategy
would be tough for us,

11:27.812 --> 11:30.023
since our point income is lower.

11:30.023 --> 11:32.483
First, we'll gather intel
at the social event.

11:32.483 --> 11:33.693
Understood.

11:33.693 --> 11:34.944
I'll help you.

11:34.944 --> 11:38.198
Oh? Are you really going
to lend me a hand?

11:38.198 --> 11:43.203
I just want to observe what kind
of strategy you'll use.

11:43.203 --> 11:44.662
I see.

11:44.662 --> 11:47.916
It may have been a bit hasty for me
to call that "cooperation."

11:52.598 --> 11:55.715
She's building relationships
based on mutual trust.

11:56.216 --> 11:58.718
That really is a very
Ichinose-like approach.

11:58.718 --> 12:01.721
But is it really okay to take it slow?

12:01.721 --> 12:04.474
Class B is going to snatch up
all the top students.

12:04.474 --> 12:06.476
Do you really think
we can beat Ichinose

12:06.476 --> 12:09.979
and the others
in a contest of social skills?

12:10.772 --> 12:12.565
I don't think so at all.

12:12.565 --> 12:13.691
Let's go.

12:15.526 --> 12:17.195
Are you sure about this, Suzune?

12:18.321 --> 12:21.740
More than half the students aren't
attending this gathering.

12:22.408 --> 12:24.744
I'll negotiate with
those students instead.

12:25.555 --> 12:26.746
Huh?

12:26.746 --> 12:28.581
Did you notice?

12:28.581 --> 12:32.377
There was a bias among the students
who showed up at the gathering.

12:32.377 --> 12:33.878
A bias?

12:33.878 --> 12:38.383
Not a single student
from Class 1-D attended.

12:38.383 --> 12:40.635
So what?
Does that mean anything?

12:40.635 --> 12:43.388
There are 40 students in a class.

12:43.388 --> 12:48.893
The fact that not even one showed up
clearly reflects the will of the class.

12:48.893 --> 12:52.230
In other words,
Class 1-D already has a leader,

12:52.230 --> 12:55.149
and that person decided
to skip the gathering.

12:55.149 --> 12:56.150
Yes.

12:56.150 --> 12:58.778
If we can negotiate
directly with that leader,

12:58.778 --> 13:01.281
there's no need to bargain
with individuals.

13:06.452 --> 13:09.789
Yo.
Ichinose is the same as ever, huh?

13:10.331 --> 13:14.419
It seems she's prioritizing saving
her classmates and the first-years.

13:14.419 --> 13:17.839
Doesn't she realize what a disadvantage
it is to take in a bunch of weak students?

13:17.839 --> 13:20.174
It's basically the same
as throwing the game.

13:20.174 --> 13:22.427
You Class A people...

13:22.427 --> 13:25.763
Sakayanagi must have
predicted this situation.

13:25.763 --> 13:27.557
That's why she didn't attend.

13:27.557 --> 13:30.059
Well, maybe.

13:30.059 --> 13:33.813
So how do you plan to bring
the top students to your side?

13:33.813 --> 13:36.983
That's all up to the princess.

13:36.983 --> 13:39.319
I'm just following her orders.

13:41.821 --> 13:43.114
Let's hurry.

13:43.114 --> 13:47.327
The Class D students should still be
in their classroom at this hour.

13:52.457 --> 13:56.002
You're pretty late, Suzune.

13:56.592 --> 13:57.837
Ryuen...

13:57.837 --> 14:01.507
Are you here to scout first-years
since you didn't attend the gathering?

14:01.507 --> 14:06.304
The ones gathered in the gym were
all just a bunch of useless idiots, right?

14:06.304 --> 14:07.513
Not even worth looking at.

14:07.513 --> 14:10.016
You've already gone after Class D?!

14:10.016 --> 14:11.809
Relax.

14:11.809 --> 14:14.020
I haven't settled on anyone yet.

14:14.020 --> 14:17.440
You'll just scare the
first-years with that attitude.

14:17.440 --> 14:22.028
I figured if I pressured them a little,
they'd agree to cooperate right away.

14:22.028 --> 14:23.571
You're joking.

14:23.571 --> 14:25.782
Do you really think that kind
of method would be allowed?

14:25.782 --> 14:28.576
Allowed or not doesn't matter.

14:28.576 --> 14:33.039
There's no rule that says forcing someone
to become your partner is forbidden.

14:33.039 --> 14:35.792
It doesn't have
to be written in the rules.

14:35.792 --> 14:38.378
If it becomes a problem,
you'll be the one in trouble.

14:38.378 --> 14:41.005
Then show me where the problem is.

14:41.005 --> 14:44.634
Well, I'm not dumb enough to do
anything that would get me caught.

14:45.885 --> 14:48.596
Let me tell you something interesting.

14:48.596 --> 14:53.393
This year's freshmen are surprisingly
calm for having just started school.

14:53.393 --> 14:55.395
What are you trying to say?

14:55.395 --> 14:58.231
It means there's
a high chance that someone...

14:58.231 --> 15:00.441
...on the school's side
has already told them

15:00.441 --> 15:04.153
how the system works,
at least to some extent.

15:05.655 --> 15:10.243
Well, do your best to keep up.

15:10.243 --> 15:15.915
The first-years might resist us much
more than I expected.

15:15.915 --> 15:19.168
Then let's start negotiating
with them right away!

15:22.296 --> 15:24.298
What the hell is going on?!

15:24.298 --> 15:28.428
This class might be
more troublesome than I thought.

15:31.931 --> 15:34.559
I don't think I can find a partner...

15:34.559 --> 15:39.279
<i>According to the notification, 22 pairs
have been finalized as of this morning.</i>

15:40.022 --> 15:44.193
<i>If I end up drawing a White Room student
as my partner, it's instant expulsion.</i>

15:44.193 --> 15:47.947
<i>For now, I've memorized the faces
and names of all the first-years...</i>

15:47.947 --> 15:49.532
H-Hey!

15:50.408 --> 15:52.535
Some first-years
are coming over here!

16:08.801 --> 16:13.014
My name is Tsubasa Nanase from Class 1-D.

16:13.014 --> 16:15.057
He's from the same class.
This is...

16:15.057 --> 16:16.809
Hosen.

16:16.809 --> 16:20.024
Coming onto the upperclassmen's floor...

16:20.025 --> 16:23.858
That's quite a bold move for new students.

16:23.858 --> 16:25.776
I admire your nerve.

16:27.028 --> 16:28.946
What do you mean,
"admire our nerve"?

16:28.946 --> 16:30.281
Don't act so high and mighty.

16:30.615 --> 16:33.784
Don't get cocky, you first-year brat!

16:35.036 --> 16:37.038
His academic score is E+.

16:37.038 --> 16:39.832
You're just as much of
an idiot as you look.

16:39.832 --> 16:41.125
What did you say?!

16:41.125 --> 16:42.376
Well, this works out.

16:42.376 --> 16:46.255
Looks like this place is crawling
with Class D rejects anyway.

16:46.255 --> 16:47.298
Works for me.

16:47.298 --> 16:49.675
What do you mean by that?

16:49.675 --> 16:52.512
You Class D guys are a bunch of losers.

16:52.512 --> 16:56.265
You can't even form pairs
unless we pick you.

16:57.558 --> 17:01.062
So we'll lend a hand
to useless idiots like you.

17:01.062 --> 17:03.064
You get what I'm saying, right?

17:03.064 --> 17:06.733
In other words,
you want to partner with us?

17:06.733 --> 17:08.819
That's quite a condescending way to ask.

17:09.779 --> 17:11.821
You're the ones who should be asking.

17:11.821 --> 17:15.826
Come on, bow your heads and say,
"Please team up with us."

17:15.826 --> 17:17.703
You bastard!

17:17.703 --> 17:19.872
It seems you're
misunderstanding something.

17:19.872 --> 17:23.584
Our positions are equal.

17:23.584 --> 17:25.169
Equal?

17:25.169 --> 17:27.255
You really don't get it.

17:27.255 --> 17:31.008
If we wanted, we'd have
plenty of ways to handle this.

17:31.008 --> 17:33.177
You don't like trouble, do you?

17:33.177 --> 17:38.558
<i>Sounds like they've realized the special
advantage that only first-years have.</i>

17:38.558 --> 17:41.269
And what kind of methods
would those be?

17:41.269 --> 17:42.853
You already know, don't you?

17:42.853 --> 17:46.774
We can intentionally
get bad test scores.

17:47.650 --> 17:49.402
That's enough already!

17:49.402 --> 17:51.529
If you mess up the exam,
you'll get kicked out of school!

17:51.529 --> 17:53.906
Yeah, if we deliberately throw the exam,
we'll get expelled.

17:53.906 --> 17:59.537
But the penalty for failing to form a pair
before the deadline is a different story.

18:00.788 --> 18:05.543
And the ones who'd be in trouble are you
second-years, who face possible expulsion.

18:05.543 --> 18:10.298
Even if you don't get expelled, are you
really willing to lose Private Points?

18:10.298 --> 18:13.843
Compared to you guys,
that's not such a big loss for us.

18:13.843 --> 18:14.635
Right?

18:14.635 --> 18:15.542
Yes.

18:15.544 --> 18:19.506
It seems we won't receive
Private Points for three months,

18:19.508 --> 18:21.726
but that's at most 240,000 points.

18:21.726 --> 18:25.146
I don't think
it would be a critical issue.

18:25.938 --> 18:28.149
Do you understand the situation now?

18:29.025 --> 18:31.444
Don't get so full of yourself!

18:31.817 --> 18:34.196
Stand down, Sudo.

18:34.196 --> 18:37.700
You understand how
this school works, don't you?

18:37.700 --> 18:38.993
I know...

18:42.079 --> 18:44.206
The only thing big about you
is your height.

18:44.206 --> 18:46.208
Just one little tap was enough?

18:46.208 --> 18:47.585
Bastard!

18:47.585 --> 18:48.961
What the hell are you doing?!

18:49.462 --> 18:50.880
Ishizaki?

18:50.880 --> 18:54.759
They just keep coming one
after another like cockroaches!

18:54.884 --> 18:55.676
Hey!

18:55.676 --> 18:58.262
You're going too far
mocking second-years!

18:58.262 --> 19:01.182
You'd better stop if you don't
want to die, Ishizaki.

19:01.182 --> 19:03.934
W-Why'd you stop me?!

19:03.934 --> 19:06.604
I know all about you.

19:06.604 --> 19:10.941
That this Hosen guy was pretty
well-known back in my hometown.

19:10.941 --> 19:14.362
Y-You know him, Ryuen?

19:14.362 --> 19:18.658
Didn't expect you
to look this dumb, though.

19:18.658 --> 19:21.285
What a coincidence.

19:21.285 --> 19:24.413
I've heard plenty about you too.

19:24.413 --> 19:29.960
Didn't expect the infamous
Ryuen to look so scrawny.

19:29.960 --> 19:33.964
You look just as much like
a musclebound freak as I imagined.

19:33.964 --> 19:37.468
I was planning to beat the hell
out of you next time I saw you,

19:37.468 --> 19:40.471
but you were probably too scared
and hiding from me, right?

19:40.971 --> 19:42.848
You got lucky.

19:42.848 --> 19:47.978
If we'd met, you wouldn't be acting
like such a big shot right now.

19:47.978 --> 19:51.562
We can settle this right here
and now if you want.

19:53.484 --> 19:54.985
I'll pass.

19:54.985 --> 19:58.739
I've got no interest in throwing hands
with a gorilla without any payoff.

19:58.739 --> 20:01.951
You're really fine with letting
a first-year disrespect you like that?

20:02.952 --> 20:05.746
I can settle things anytime.

20:05.746 --> 20:08.499
Wait.
Let's play, Ryuen.

20:08.499 --> 20:10.499
I said I'll pass.

20:10.501 --> 20:11.252
Then...

20:16.463 --> 20:17.800
Try and break free!

20:17.800 --> 20:19.051
Watch this!

20:19.051 --> 20:21.137
Come at me all at once if you want!

20:21.137 --> 20:22.471
Are you out of your mind?!

20:22.471 --> 20:23.973
You!

20:23.973 --> 20:26.100
Hosen, please stop.

20:27.476 --> 20:29.017
The upperclassmen have been concerned

20:29.019 --> 20:31.019
about the surveillance cameras
for a while now.

20:31.021 --> 20:34.650
I've determined there's no benefit
to causing trouble here.

20:34.650 --> 20:37.611
I know that.
I'm just having a little fun.

20:37.611 --> 20:41.782
If you continue this pointless behavior,
I have an idea of my own.

20:41.782 --> 20:45.035
I'm considering bringing <i>that</i> up
and telling everyone here about it.

20:50.916 --> 20:54.378
So you're threatening me, Nanase?

20:54.378 --> 20:58.382
But if you disappoint me,
I won't go easy on you.

20:58.382 --> 21:00.885
I'll take whatever comes my way.

21:00.885 --> 21:03.387
What are you doing, Hosen?

21:04.638 --> 21:07.641
Fights aren't something you
start out with in the open.

21:07.641 --> 21:10.561
This isn't even a fight.

21:10.561 --> 21:12.229
Let's go, Nanase.

21:12.229 --> 21:13.898
That is a wise choice.

21:19.153 --> 21:23.783
If you get on your knees and beg, I might
even pair up with you, Senior Ryuen.

21:23.783 --> 21:27.578
Too bad. I've got no interest
in teaming up with a wild gorilla.

21:27.578 --> 21:29.580
That's a shame.

21:30.831 --> 21:32.458
See you, Horikita.

21:35.294 --> 21:37.087
We apologize for the disturbance.

21:49.600 --> 21:52.812
<i>That probing gaze...</i>

22:17.169 --> 22:18.712
<i>You're the outcast monster</i>

22:18.712 --> 22:21.841
<i>Hidin', hidin', ding-ding-ding</i>

22:21.841 --> 22:23.717
<i>Hidin', hidin'</i>

22:25.344 --> 22:31.267
<i>Hidin', hidin', ding-ding-ding</i>

22:31.851 --> 22:35.145
<i>Tell me what a genius is</i>

22:35.145 --> 22:38.065
<i>The one who creates singularities</i>

22:38.065 --> 22:41.402
<i>Don't you feed the monster</i>

22:41.402 --> 22:47.491
<i>The black outline has gotten bigger again</i>

22:47.491 --> 22:51.579
<i>Everyone is always the king's piece</i>

22:51.579 --> 22:54.371
<i>Hidin', hidin', ding-ding-ding</i>

22:54.373 --> 22:58.127
<i>How does it feel to see into the future?</i>

22:58.127 --> 23:02.298
<i>A monster made of lies</i>

23:02.298 --> 23:06.385
<i>The monster hidin, hidin',
go get him</i>

23:06.385 --> 23:09.638
<i>A twisted, unfair trigger</i>

23:09.638 --> 23:13.178
<i>Hidin', hidin', the maze thoughts</i>

23:13.180 --> 23:16.270
<i>Mysteries swimming in them</i>

23:16.270 --> 23:18.689
<i>Confused by truth and lies</i>

23:18.689 --> 23:22.484
<i>Are we the prey or the hunters?</i>

23:22.484 --> 23:24.320
<i>Hidin', hidin'</i>

23:24.320 --> 23:25.738
<i>Longing for the ordinary</i>

23:25.738 --> 23:27.573
<i>Hidin', hidin'</i>

23:27.573 --> 23:29.366
<i>Yes, you are the outcast monster</i>

23:29.366 --> 23:35.956
<i>Hidin', hidin', ding-ding-ding</i>
